---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Lesson 13: Display Devices - C00lnerd Tutoring">
    <div style="max-width: 1000px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 2rem;">
        
        <!-- Header -->
        <div style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #667eea 0%, #764ba2 100%); color: white; padding: 3rem; border-radius: 15px; margin-bottom: 2rem; text-align: center;">
            <h1 style="font-size: 2.5rem; font-weight: bold; margin: 0 0 1rem 0;">Lesson 13: Display Devices</h1>
            <p style="font-size: 1.2rem; margin: 0 0 1rem 0; opacity: 0.9;">Put your robot arm's status on a real screen — no laptop required</p>
            <div style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.2); padding: 1rem; border-radius: 10px; display: inline-block;">
                <p style="margin: 0; font-weight: 600;">📺 Serial Monitor was a window into the chip. Now the chip talks back on its own screen.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Learning Objectives -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">🎯 Learning Objectives</h2>
            
            <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 2rem;">
                <div>
                    <h4 style="color: #2d3748; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">By the end of this lesson, you will:</h4>
                    <ul style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8; margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem;">
                        <li>Wire a 16×2 character LCD with an I2C backpack using only 4 wires</li>
                        <li>Print text and numbers with the <code>LiquidCrystal_I2C</code> library</li>
                        <li>Show live servo angles and the arm's control mode on the screen</li>
                        <li>Refresh a display without flicker or <code>delay()</code></li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h4 style="color: #2d3748; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Key Concepts:</h4>
                    <ul style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8; margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem;">
                        <li>I2C bus — two shared wires, address instead of pins</li>
                        <li>Cursor positioning and fixed-width formatting</li>
                        <li>Timed screen updates with <code>millis()</code></li>
                        <li>UI design on a tiny screen (16 characters per line)</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 1: Parts Inventory -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">📦 Section 1: Parts Inventory</h2>
            
            <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 2rem;">
                <div style="background: #e6fffa; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #2d3748; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">New this lesson</h4>
                    <ul style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8; margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem;">
                        <li>1 × 16×2 character LCD <strong>with I2C backpack</strong> soldered on (look for the small chip and 4-pin header on the back)</li>
                        <li>4 × female-to-male jumper wires</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div style="background: #f0f9ff; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #2d3748; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Carried over from Lesson 12</h4>
                    <ul style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8; margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem;">
                        <li>Arduino Uno + USB cable</li>
                        <li>Joystick module + 2 SG90 servos</li>
                        <li>Breadboard and jumper wires</li>
                        <li>The whole working joystick control sketch</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div style="background: #fef3c7; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; margin-top: 1.5rem; border-left: 4px solid #f59e0b;">
                <h4 style="color: #92400e; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">💡 Why I2C?</h4>
                <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
                    A bare 16×2 LCD needs <strong>six</strong> Arduino pins just for data and control. The little I2C backpack chip on the back talks over a shared two-wire bus instead — we spend zero digital pins, and the joystick and servos keep every pin they already had.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Pin Map -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">📌 Section 2: Pin Map &amp; Wiring</h2>
            
            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                The backpack has just four pins: <strong>GND</strong>, <strong>VCC</strong>, <strong>SDA</strong> (data), and <strong>SCL</strong> (clock). On the Uno, the I2C bus lives on the last two analog pins:
            </p>

            <div style="background: #f0f9ff; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #2d3748; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Complete robot-arm pin map</h4>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem; color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.9;">
                    <li><strong>LCD SDA:</strong> A4 (I2C data)</li>
                    <li><strong>LCD SCL:</strong> A5 (I2C clock)</li>
                    <li><strong>LCD VCC / GND:</strong> power rail / ground rail</li>
                    <li><strong>Joystick VRx / VRy / SW:</strong> A0 / A1 / A3 (unchanged)</li>
                    <li><strong>Button:</strong> A2, <strong>Buzzer (optional):</strong> pin 8</li>
                    <li><strong>Base servo:</strong> pin 9, <strong>Shoulder servo:</strong> pin 10</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-top: 1.5rem;">
                There's also a tiny <strong>contrast potentiometer</strong> (blue box with a screw) on the backpack, and usually a backlight jumper. If you see a blank screen later, that screw is the first thing to turn.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Hello Display -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">👋 Section 3: Hello, Display</h2>
            
            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                First install the library: <strong>Sketch → Include Library → Manage Libraries</strong>, search <code>LiquidCrystal_I2C</code>, and install it (the <em>Frank de Brabander</em> / <em>marcoschwartz</em> version). Then prove the wiring with the classic first test:
            </p>

            <div style="position: relative;">
                <button onclick="copyCode(this)" style="position: absolute; top: 8px; right: 8px; background-color: #2563eb; color: white; border: none; padding: 4px 8px; border-radius: 4px; cursor: pointer; font-size: 12px; z-index: 10;">📋 Copy</button>
                <pre style="background: #1e293b; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1.25rem; border-radius: 10px; overflow-x: auto; font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.6;"><code>{`#include <Wire.h>
#include <LiquidCrystal_I2C.h>

// Most backpacks answer at 0x27; if your screen stays
// blank, try 0x3F instead.
LiquidCrystal_I2C lcd(0x27, 16, 2);   // address, columns, rows

void setup() {
    lcd.init();          // wake the backpack up
    lcd.backlight();     // turn the light on
    lcd.setCursor(0, 0); // column 0, row 0 (top line)
    lcd.print("Hello, arm!");
    lcd.setCursor(0, 1); // second line
    lcd.print("Base: ?  Mode: ?");
}

void loop() {
    // nothing yet — text stays until overwritten
}`}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div style="background: #fef2f2; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; margin-top: 1.5rem; border-left: 4px solid #dc2626;">
                <h4 style="color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">⚠️ Blank screen checklist</h4>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem; color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8;">
                    <li><strong>Squares but no text?</strong> Wrong address — change <code>0x27</code> to <code>0x3F</code> in the constructor.</li>
                    <li><strong>Nothing at all?</strong> Twist the little blue contrast screw until faint blocks appear.</li>
                    <li><strong>Still nothing?</strong> Swap SDA/SCL — they're the most commonly reversed pair.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Live data -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">📊 Section 4: Live Data Without Flicker</h2>
            
            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                Two rookie mistakes make LCDs painful. First, calling <code>lcd.print()</code> every <code>loop()</code> — the screen redraws thousands of times a second and shimmers. Fix: update on the same <code>millis()</code> timer pattern you already know, every 250 ms:
            </p>

            <div style="position: relative;">
                <button onclick="copyCode(this)" style="position: absolute; top: 8px; right: 8px; background-color: #2563eb; color: white; border: none; padding: 4px 8px; border-radius: 4px; cursor: pointer; font-size: 12px; z-index: 10;">📋 Copy</button>
                <pre style="background: #1e293b; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1.25rem; border-radius: 10px; overflow-x: auto; font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.6;"><code>{`unsigned long lastDisplayUpdate = 0;
const int displayInterval = 250;   // refresh 4x per second

void updateDisplay() {
    lcd.setCursor(0, 0);
    lcd.print("B:");
    pad3(baseAngle);          // always 3 chars wide
    lcd.print("  S:");
    pad3(shoulderAngle);
    lcd.print("   ");         // wipe any leftover characters

    lcd.setCursor(0, 1);
    lcd.print("Mode: MANUAL   ");
}

// Print a number padded to 3 columns so 45 doesn't
// leave a ghost digit where 145 used to sit
void pad3(int n) {
    if (n < 100) lcd.print(" ");
    if (n < 10)  lcd.print(" ");
    lcd.print(n);
}`}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-top: 1.5rem;">
                Second mistake: <code>lcd.clear()</code> every update — that wipes the whole screen each time, which is what actually causes visible flicker. We never clear; we <strong>overwrite fixed-width fields</strong>. The old characters simply get replaced.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Complete sketch -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">🧩 Section 5: The Complete Sketch</h2>
            
            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                Lesson 12's joystick control, now reporting on the LCD. Watch for <strong>three</strong> independent <code>millis()</code> timers running at once — motion every 15 ms, display every 250 ms — proof that non-blocking code really does let one chip juggle several jobs:
            </p>

            <div style="position: relative;">
                <button onclick="copyCode(this)" style="position: absolute; top: 8px; right: 8px; background-color: #2563eb; color: white; border: none; padding: 4px 8px; border-radius: 4px; cursor: pointer; font-size: 12px; z-index: 10;">📋 Copy</button>
                <pre style="background: #1e293b; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1.25rem; border-radius: 10px; overflow-x: auto; font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.6;"><code>{`// ============================================================
// Lesson 13: Display Devices
// Joystick-driven arm with live status on a 16x2 I2C LCD.
//   X -> base (pin 9)   Y -> shoulder (pin 10)
//   SW click -> home   LCD -> angles + mode
// ============================================================

#include <Servo.h>
#include <Wire.h>
#include <LiquidCrystal_I2C.h>

Servo baseServo;
Servo shoulderServo;
LiquidCrystal_I2C lcd(0x27, 16, 2);   // try 0x3F if blank

// ---- Pins ----
const int joyX  = A0;
const int joyY  = A1;
const int joySW = A3;

// ---- Servo state ----
int baseAngle     = 90;
int shoulderAngle = 90;
const int MIN_ANGLE = 20;
const int MAX_ANGLE = 160;
const char modeText[] = "MANUAL";   // future: AUTO, etc.

// ---- Joystick calibration ----
int centerX = 512;
int centerY = 512;
const int deadZone = 25;

// ---- Two independent timers ----
unsigned long lastStepTime    = 0;
unsigned long lastDisplayTime = 0;
const int stepInterval    = 15;    // servo update every 15 ms
const int displayInterval = 250;   // screen update every 250 ms
const int maxStep = 4;

int lastSWState = HIGH;

int signOf(int v) {
    return (v > 0) - (v < 0);
}

int stepFor(int deflection) {
    int d = abs(deflection);
    if (d < deadZone) return 0;
    return map(d, deadZone, 512, 1, maxStep);
}

void calibrateJoystick() {
    long sumX = 0, sumY = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < 50; i++) {
        sumX += analogRead(joyX);
        sumY += analogRead(joyY);
        delay(10);
    }
    centerX = sumX / 50;
    centerY = sumY / 50;
}

// Print a 3-digit-padded angle (avoids ghost digits)
void pad3(int n) {
    if (n < 100) lcd.print(" ");
    if (n < 10)  lcd.print(" ");
    lcd.print(n);
}

void updateDisplay() {
    // Line 1:  B: 90  S: 45
    lcd.setCursor(0, 0);
    lcd.print("B:");
    pad3(baseAngle);
    lcd.print((char)223);   // degree symbol on HD44780
    lcd.print(" S:");
    pad3(shoulderAngle);
    lcd.print((char)223);

    // Line 2:  Mode: MANUAL
    lcd.setCursor(0, 1);
    lcd.print("Mode: ");
    lcd.print(modeText);
    lcd.print("        ");  // wipe leftover chars
}

void setup() {
    baseServo.attach(9);
    shoulderServo.attach(10);
    pinMode(joySW, INPUT_PULLUP);
    baseServo.write(baseAngle);
    shoulderServo.write(shoulderAngle);

    lcd.init();
    lcd.backlight();
    lcd.setCursor(0, 0);
    lcd.print("Calibrating...");
    lcd.setCursor(0, 1);
    lcd.print("Hands off stick");

    calibrateJoystick();
    updateDisplay();
}

void loop() {
    // Job 1: timed servo motion (every 15 ms)
    if (millis() - lastStepTime >= stepInterval) {
        lastStepTime = millis();
        int dx = analogRead(joyX) - centerX;
        int dy = analogRead(joyY) - centerY;
        baseAngle     = constrain(baseAngle     + signOf(dx) * stepFor(dx), MIN_ANGLE, MAX_ANGLE);
        shoulderAngle = constrain(shoulderAngle + signOf(dy) * stepFor(dy), MIN_ANGLE, MAX_ANGLE);
        baseServo.write(baseAngle);
        shoulderServo.write(shoulderAngle);
    }

    // Job 2: timed screen refresh (every 250 ms)
    if (millis() - lastDisplayTime >= displayInterval) {
        lastDisplayTime = millis();
        updateDisplay();
    }

    // Job 3: button check (every pass — no timer needed)
    int swState = digitalRead(joySW);
    if (swState == LOW && lastSWState == HIGH) {
        baseAngle = 90;
        shoulderAngle = 90;
    }
    lastSWState = swState;
}`}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div style="background: #ecfdf5; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; margin-top: 1.5rem; border-left: 4px solid #059669;">
                <h4 style="color: #065f46; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">🔍 The architecture lesson</h4>
                <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
                    Each job in <code>loop()</code> is a small "if enough time passed, do a little work" guard. Motion stays smooth while the screen redraws, and button presses are caught instantly — something impossible with a big <code>delay()</code> anywhere in the loop. This pattern scales to every sensor you'll add later.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Tuning & challenges -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">🔧 Section 6: Tuning and Next Steps</h2>
            
            <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 1rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
                <div style="background: #fef2f2; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Screen lags behind the arm</h4>
                    <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; font-size: 0.9rem;">By design — the screen only refreshes 4×/second. Lower <code>displayInterval</code> to 100 for snappier updates, but don't go below ~50 or I2C traffic steals loop time.</p>
                </div>
                <div style="background: #fef2f2; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Ghost digits (90 → 920)</h4>
                    <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; font-size: 0.9rem;">A shorter number didn't overwrite the old one. That's why <code>pad3()</code> exists — never <code>lcd.clear()</code>, always overwrite fixed-width.</p>
                </div>
                <div style="background: #fef2f2; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Random garbled characters</h4>
                    <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; font-size: 0.9rem;">Servo current spikes corrupt I2C. Power servos from a separate 5V supply (shared GND!) and keep SDA/SCL wires short.</p>
                </div>
                <div style="background: #fef2f2; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Display works then freezes</h4>
                    <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; font-size: 0.9rem;">A <code>delay()</code> snuck back in somewhere, or <code>updateDisplay()</code> runs every loop instead of on its timer.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div style="background: #f0f9ff; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #0ea5e9;">
                <h4 style="color: #0c4a6e; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">🛠️ Put It Together — Challenges</h4>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem; color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.9;">
                    <li><strong>Add the elbow:</strong> a 16×2 only has 2 lines. Make the screen alternate: page 1 shows B/S, page 2 shows E/mode, switching every 2 seconds on a third timer.</li>
                    <li><strong>Bar graph:</strong> fill row 2 with <code>#</code> characters proportional to joystick deflection — a live input meter.</li>
                    <li><strong>Mode toggle:</strong> wire Lesson 11's A2 button to switch <code>modeText</code> between "MANUAL" and "HOME" and watch the display update.</li>
                    <li><strong>Got a 20×4 display?</strong> It's the same library — change the constructor to <code>(0x27, 20, 4)</code> and put each value on its own line: Base / Shoulder / Elbow / Mode.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div style="text-center; margin-top: 1.5rem;">
                <a href="/programming/quizzes32/lesson13" style="background-color: #7c3aed; color: white; padding: 12px 24px; border-radius: 8px; text-decoration: none; font-weight: 600; border: 2px solid #6d28d9; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1); transition: all 0.2s; display: inline-block;" onmouseover="this.style.backgroundColor='#6d28d9'; this.style.transform='translateY(-2px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 10px 15px -3px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'" onmouseout="this.style.backgroundColor='#7c3aed'; this.style.transform='translateY(0px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'">
                    Take Lesson 13 Quiz →
                </a>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Navigation -->
        <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; margin-top: 2rem;">
            <div>
